          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            What
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            is for
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+theory"} (∞,1)-category theory
            %em directed homotopy type theory
            is (or should be) for
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+homotopy+theory"} directed homotopy theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-category"} (∞,n)-category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} theory
          %p More in detail: Where
          %ul
            %li
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
              have
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation+between+category+theory+and+type+theory"} interpretation
              as
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} ∞-groupoids
              aka
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C0%29-categories"} (∞,0)-categories
              (and more generally: as
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-stacks"} ∞-stacks
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
              itself is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+language"} internal language
              for the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
              (specifically, for
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence+axiom"} univalent HoTT
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} (∞,1)-toposes
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C0%29-categories"} (∞,0)-categories
              form
          %p so
          %ul
            %li
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
              of directed homotopy type theory ought to have
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation+between+type+theory+and+category+theory"} interpretation
              as
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-categories"} (∞,n)-categories
              for some
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ,
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \{1, 2, \cdots, \infty\}
              (and more generally: as
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-sheaves"} (∞,n+1)-sheaves
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+language"} internal language
              for the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-category"} (∞,n+1)-categories
              (and in the suitably directed
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence+axiom"} univalent
              case:
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-toposes"} (∞,n+1)-toposes
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-categories"} (∞,n)-categories
              form.
          %p
            A proposal for the case
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = \infty
            (potentially describing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C%E2%88%9E%29-categories"} (∞,∞)-categories
            aka
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/omega-categories"} omega-categories
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opetopic+type+theory"} opetopic type theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#opetopic+type+theory#Finster12"} Finster (2012)
          %p
            A proposal for the case
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 1
            with the directed analog of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence+axiom"} univalence axiom
            included — hence for a type theory whose types may be interpreted as
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C2%29-sheaves"} (∞,2)-sheaves
            and which itself would be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+language"} internal language
            of the
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C2%29-category"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,2)
                \-categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C2%29-topos"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,2)
              \-toposes
            that these form — is announced in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#CisinskiEtAl23"} Cisinski et al. 2023
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#CCNW25"} 2025
          %h2#appeal_and_practicality Appeal and practicality
          %p
            One obvious reason for studying directed homotopy type theory (especially when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = \infty
            in the above) is generality:
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,\infty)
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em Universe types can retain their higher directed structure.
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+universe"} Universe types
                are (small) ‘internal’ reflections of the category of all types of our type theory, but without sufficient higher structure in the theory, this reflection process must forget structure present in the external category (for instance, the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sets"} category of sets
                can only contain a ‘set of sets’ as an object which does not remember functions; similarly, the universe type in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
                must forget about maps between types that are not equivalences).
            %li
              %p
                %em Dependent function types become ‘just’ function types.
                In the presence of higher directed structure, equipped with a universe type
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒰
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
                and unit type
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{1}
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Π
                          %mrow
                            %mi x
                            %mo :
                            %mi A
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_{x : A} F(x)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                a type family
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                        %mo :
                        %mi A
                        %mo →
                        %mi 𝒰
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : A \to \mathcal{U}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                          %mi Fun
                        %mrow
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                            %mi Fun
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi A
                          %mo ,
                          %mi 𝒰
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} const
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathsf{Fun}_{\mathsf{Fun}(A,\mathcal{U})}(\mathrm{const}_{\mathbf{1}}, F)
                (where
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} const
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mn 1
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi 𝒰
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{const}_{\mathbf{1}} : A \to \mathcal{U}
                is the constant functor with image
                = succeed ")." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{1}
            %li
              %p
                %em Inductive types become substantially more expressive.
                Inductive types with dependent constructors can also be expressed in terms of non-dependent constructors: for instance, for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                a type family
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                        %mo :
                        %mi A
                        %mo →
                        %mi 𝒰
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : A \to \mathcal{U}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Σ
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo :
                          %mi A
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_{x : A} F(x)
                can be introduced with a single constructor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} in
                      %mo :
                      %mi F
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} const
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi Σ
                            %mrow
                              %mi x
                              %mo :
                              %mi A
                          %mi F
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{in} : F \to \mathrm{const}_{\Sigma_{x : A} F(x)}
                (which is a map in the function type
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                        %mi Fun
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi 𝒰
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathsf{Fun}(A, \mathcal{U})
                and should thus be thought of as a natural transformation between functors). Moreover, there is the topic of
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+inductive+types"} higher inductive types
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
                type
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mo ,
                      %mo ≤
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbb{N},\leq)
                with constructors
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo :
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mn 1
                      %mo →
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 : \mathbf{1} \to \mathbb{N}
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                        %mi succ
                      %mo :
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mo →
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathsf{succ} : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                        %mi less
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} id
                        %mi ℕ
                      %mo →
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                        %mi succ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathsf{less} : \id_{\mathbb{N}} \to \mathsf{succ}
            %li
              %p
                %em Formalization of (higher) categorical semantics.
                On a more practical side, access to higher directed structure could allow us to formalize the categorical semantics of other types theories, such as
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/directed+homotopy+type+theory#CisinskiEtAl23"} Cisinki et al 2023
          %p On the other hand, working directed higher type theory may not be very practical.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                While it is clear that one eventually wants to speak about higher categorical concepts with type theory, it is not a priori clear that this motivates the dedicated formulation of new rules for directed higher type theory: it might still be more convenient to instead work internal to ordinary
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            %li
              %p More generally, it has been argued that directed higher type theories may not aid the practical usability of proof assistants due to the potential complexity of the rules involved.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+univalence+axiom"} directed univalence axiom
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+type+theory"}> directed type theory
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-type+theory"} 2-type theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-logic"} 2-logic
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opetopic+type+theory"} opetopic type theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semi-simplicial+type"} semi-simplicial type
            %li
              %p
